so anthony trelope he was a writer  who published 47 novels 18 works of  nonfiction  12 short stories two plays  and a series of um articles and letters  he wrote in 15 minute intervals for  three hours  every single day he would write 250  words  every 15 minutes and after he was done  that he would take a little break  as sad as this may sound we are all  results driven creatures  and personally i love just  checking off that little box off of my  task list let's say i'm writing an essay  and it takes days or weeks and on my  task list  i just have write an essay for a really  long time i'm not going to  be able to get that satisfaction of  completing a task  at the end of the day honestly i'm not  going to feel that accomplished because  i didn't check off anything on my to-do  list  in that case i would just break it up  into smaller tasks you know i would  say okay you're gonna write 200 words  and i would give myself a time limit and  then just pump out those 200 words  whether it's good or bad just do it  and then later on you can give yourself  a task to review that work  and edit it when you complete smaller  tasks it builds momentum  and it adds fuel for for the long run  also when you complete a meaningful task  quickly it energizes you  and it motivates you to do more tasks  quickly and so i would challenge you  to try to schedule your most important  tasks right in the morning  and obviously break it up into little  chunks that's manageable so that you can  do it within the time that you have in  the morning  but i think it'll really help you build  confidence  and energize you for the rest of the day  because it definitely works for me  you
 

